Toronto Mobility Analytics — Project Rule
Project Identity

This repository contains a portfolio project focused primarily on data analytics and business intelligence, with supporting data engineering work.

The project uses TTC and Bike Share Toronto data to investigate how current mobility conditions compare with historical mobility patterns.

The project is intended to be the developer's first substantial data project. The developer has a computer science background but is still learning practical data analytics, BI, data engineering, databases, APIs, and data pipelines.

Core Analytical Thesis

The central analytical idea is:

Understand typical mobility patterns using historical data, then use live/current data to determine how current conditions compare with those historical patterns.

The project should not become a generic transportation dashboard.

Historical data provides context and baselines.

Live/current data provides observations that can be compared against that context.

The relationship is:

Historical Data
      ↓
Typical Mobility Patterns
      ↓
Historical Baselines
      ↓
Current Data
      ↓
Current vs Historical Comparison
      ↓
Identify Meaningful Deviations

Primary Analytical Questions

The project should remain centered around two connected questions.

Question 1

How do mobility patterns vary across time and location for TTC and Bike Share Toronto?

This establishes historical context.

Relevant dimensions may include:

Time of day

Day of week

Weekday versus weekend

Date/month/season where appropriate

Location

Station

Route where supported

Transportation system

Question 2

How do current TTC and Bike Share conditions compare with their historical patterns, and where are the largest deviations occurring?

This connects the historical analysis to live/current data.

The project should investigate:

Current conditions

Historical expectations

Current versus historical differences

Locations with unusual conditions

Time periods with unusual conditions

Magnitude and persistence of deviations where supported

The exact methodology must be determined from the actual available data.

Data Sources

The project focuses on:

TTC

Bike Share Toronto

GO Transit is outside the initial scope.

Do not assume that a desired field, metric, API, or historical dataset exists.

Before designing major parts of the system, investigate the actual public data and documentation.

Historical and Live Data

Both historical and current/live data are important.

Historical data should be used to:

Identify recurring patterns

Establish baselines

Understand temporal behavior

Understand spatial behavior

Provide context

Live/current data should be used to:

Capture current conditions

Demonstrate repeatable extraction

Compare current observations with historical expectations

Support future refreshes

Live data should have analytical purpose rather than existing only as a technical demonstration.

Analytics-First Scope

This is primarily a data analytics / BI project.

Data engineering is intentionally included as supporting infrastructure.

Prioritize:

Analytical validity

Data quality

Meaningful metrics

Clear visual communication

Appropriate data engineering

Technical sophistication

Do not introduce engineering complexity merely to make the project appear more advanced.

Interactive Visualization

An interactive map may be used when it supports the analytical questions.

Potential uses include:

Bike Share station locations

Current station conditions

Historical station patterns

Current versus historical deviations

Spatial investigation

Do not add a map merely because the developer has a computer science/frontend background.

Every major visualization should have an analytical purpose.

Station-Level Analysis

Bike Share station-level investigation is an important potential analytical component.

Where supported by the data, a station detail view may show:

Current conditions

Historical typical conditions

Current versus historical differences

Historical patterns by hour

Historical patterns by day

Recent observations

Geographic context

The actual metrics must be based on the available data.

Data Engineering

The project should include practical data engineering work without becoming a dedicated data engineering project.

Potential components include:

API/feed extraction

Live/frequently updated ingestion

Raw data storage

Data validation

Timestamp handling

Cleaning

Transformation

Deduplication

Analytical database/storage

Repeatable refresh processes

The architecture should remain proportional to a first data project.

Data Investigation Before Architecture

Do not finalize major architecture decisions before investigating the actual data.

When encountering a data source, determine:

What it represents

Available fields

Historical coverage

Update frequency

Timestamp format

Time zone

Identifiers

Geographic information

Data limitations

Reliability

Whether it supports the analytical questions

Clearly distinguish verified information from assumptions.

AI Collaboration

The developer is using Cursor as an active learning and implementation partner.

Cursor is allowed to:

Write code

Modify files

Implement features

Suggest architecture

Debug code

Generate tests

Research technical approaches

Do not refuse to provide code merely because the developer is learning.

However, the developer must understand significant implementation decisions and resulting code.

Teaching Behavior

When introducing an unfamiliar concept, explain:

What it is

Why this project needs it

How it fits into the system

Important alternatives or tradeoffs

How the implementation works

The level of explanation should match the complexity of the concept.

Avoid unnecessary lectures about concepts the developer already understands.

Progressive Assistance

When the developer is trying to solve a learning problem, use the Manware learning workflows where appropriate.

Depending on the situation, provide:

A hint

A question that guides the developer

An explanation

A partial solution

A complete implementation

Do not artificially withhold code when the developer explicitly asks for implementation.

Important Decisions

For significant analytical or architectural decisions, do not silently make assumptions.

Explicitly identify decisions involving:

Historical baselines

Deviation definitions

Aggregations

Time windows

Data cleaning

Missing values

Database design

Pipeline architecture

BI metrics

Visualization choices

Explain the relevant tradeoffs before or alongside implementation.

Plan Mode

Before major implementation begins, use Cursor Plan mode.

The initial plan should:

Read the project documentation.

Understand the analytical thesis.

Understand the two analytical questions.

Inspect the current repository.

Investigate the actual data sources.

Identify technical requirements.

Propose an implementation architecture.

Identify assumptions and uncertainties.

Identify risks and data limitations.

Produce a staged implementation plan.

Do not begin substantial implementation during the initial planning phase unless explicitly requested.

The plan is a proposal and should be reviewed before implementation.

Understanding Requirement

After substantial implementation, the developer should be able to explain:

What was built

Why it was built

How data flows through it

Why important technical decisions were made

What assumptions exist

How the system can fail

How it can be tested

How it supports the analytical questions

The developer does not need to memorize every line of generated code.

Project Decision Principle

When choosing between multiple technically valid approaches, prefer the approach that:

Supports the analytical questions

Is appropriate for the available data

Is understandable to the developer

Is maintainable

Demonstrates relevant skills

Avoids unnecessary complexity

The goal is a strong analytics/BI project with meaningful data engineering exposure, not maximum technical complexity.

